e sourced review. Never add unsupported citations, classify text\nautomatically, or label supporting evidence irrelevant merely to satisfy coverage.\nAn empty slot does not resolve an earlier issue. Do not assert hypothetical\nwhole-corpus exhaustiveness requirements; scoped sufficient evidence may resolve\na missing-evidence issue without having read every memory.\n\nWhen source_span_columns is present, source_spans contains row arrays. Match each\nrow position to source_span_columns to reconstruct every span object before\nreading. References to source_spans[].id or other fields mean those objects.\nThe id column holds source aliases (sN); doc_id holds the original document ID or,\nwhen source_documents is present, its metadata reference (dN), never source_ids.\n\nWEB SOURCE CHECK: exact entity/event/relation needs originals; audit them.\nPublication/revision/effective/event dates differ; people/orgs aren't places.\nMenu/topic labels and adjacent text prove no relation.\nAnother project's dates cannot transfer without source proof.\npartial/unknown: real incomplete evidence within the requested scope.\nEmpty ordinary retrieval uses\nno_relevant_evidence. Existing obligations still require legal model review of originals.\n\nREQUIRED WEB RETRIEVAL PROTOCOL: every requested slot declares reading_status;\nBOTH statuses require unresolved=[]. no_relevant_evidence means NO NEW admissible\nobservation: observations=[], no review/resolution IDs. Optional search_gaps lists\nuntried directions, only with this status. Old facts/obligations stay unchanged in\ncontroller state; no need to repeat them. This search result is never a negative\nfact or proof of absence. Do not cite an answer-free window as unknown; continue\nthe local bridge. evidence_assessment supplies NEW original-source-cited ordinary\nobservations. A genuine incomplete statement/ambiguity/conflict requires a named\npartial/unknown row with exact source_ids, requested scope and reason; unknown\nuses value=null. Never replace it with bare slot.unresolved or a resolution claim.\nOld evidence obligations, failed drafts and counters remain mandatory. To settle\nan offered issue, use evidence_assessment, explicitly review its original text\nwith sourced support/corrective/irrelevant observations, cover EVERY required\noriginal source, and claim the offered resolution ID. Genuinely irrelevant text\nmay settle an earlier absence-only issue; never relabel real partial facts,\nsupporting text or counters irrelevant just to close them. Empty slots and\npartial/unknown observations cannot settle evidence obligations: omit their IDs\nfrom resolved_obligation_ids. Correcting a failed draft need not settle a source\nissue. Preserve every required failed draft ID. Frozen rows, including partial/\nunknown, must not be restated. Repairs may instead use currently offered\nreviewed_observation_ids for immutable sourced rows, under all common source and\nresolution checks. Terminal composition/audit use the ordinary protocol without\nreading_status/search_gaps.\n\nweb_source_review contains exact already-visible originals and read-only links to\nprior judgments/issues/current frozen offers; it adds no evidence or permission.\ndocument_scope_review pairs requested_scopes with each document's earliest visible\noriginal, which may be mid-document. card_original_source_id refers only to this\nrequest's cards[].original_source.id. Establish the requested entity identity and\nevent/relation from offered originals before transferring any attribute. Shared\nsite, partner, topic or question context alone establishes no identity bridge.\nIn an ordinary read, compare new originals with existing requested-slot bindings:\nmatch the requested entity, event/relation and scope before taking an attribute.\nAn associated workflow, version or process can describe a different event.\nknown_observations marked model_judgment are prior model assessments, not semantic\ncertification of their source entailment. Reconsider them against the originals;\ndo not assume either the old or the new statement is correct. If a real conflict\nwarrants correction, use the existing legal contradiction/retract_ids mechanism\nand separate replacement support only where this request permits it. A review\ncard itself never retracts or changes a value. During repair, retain the offered\nedit scope and immutable current-batch rows. Only offered frozen references may\nbe used; each covers its own source_ids only. Support from a new original does\nnot review an old obligated original. Claim an obligation only after warranted\nsettled review of every required original; otherwise leave it pending. Do not\ninvent agreement or call genuine partial/counter evidence irrelevant to close it.\n\n\nReturn one complete JSON object matching this schema:\n{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"audit_verdict\":{\"enum\":[\"supported\",\"unknown\",\"conflict\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"final_prediction\":{\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]},\"semantic_answer\":{},\"slots\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"allOf\":[{\"else\":{\"anyOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"minItems\":1}}}],\"not\":{\"required\":[\"search_gaps\"]}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"reading_status\":{\"const\":\"no_relevant_evidence\"}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"unresolved\":{\"maxItems\":0}}}}],\"oneOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"allOf\":[{\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\"]}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"const\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\"},\"time_scope\":{\"const\":\"agreement\"}}}}],\"else\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"null\"}}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"const\":\"support\"}}},\"properties\":{\"retract_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"]}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}}}}},\"variable\":{\"const\":\"location\"}}}],\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"parent_versions\":{\"additionalProperties\":{\"type\":\"integer\"},\"type\":\"object\"},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"retract_ids\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"source_ids\":{\"description\":\"Exact visible source_spans[].id aliases, such as s3; never document IDs or invented IDs.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"time_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"value\":{}},\"required\":[\"id\",\"stance\",\"value\",\"source_ids\",\"entity_scope\",\"time_scope\",\"parent_versions\",\"retract_ids\",\"reason\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"reading_status\":{\"enum\":[\"no_relevant_evidence\",\"evidence_assessment\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"search_gaps\":{\"description\":\"Untried search directions only; permitted with empty observations and unresolved.\",\"items\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"},\"unresolved\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"maxItems\":0,\"type\":\"array\"},\"variable\":{\"enum\":[\"location\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"variable\",\"observations\",\"unresolved\",\"reading_status\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"maxItems\":1,\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"}},\"required\":[\"slots\"],\"type\":\"object\"}"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"web_source_review\":{\"read_only\":true,\"grants_review_authority\":false,\"existing_permissions_unchanged\":true,\"document_scope_review\":{\"requested_scopes\":{\"location\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"documents\":[{\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"earliest_visible_original\":{\"id\":\"s35\",\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"start\":0,\"end\":366,\"text\":\"http://www.icse-conferences.org/sc/ICSE/2019/ICSE2019-PC-Chairs-Report.pdf\\nICSE 2019 PC Chairs’ Report Tevfik Bultan and Jon Whittle, ICSE 2019 PC Co-Chairs  Executive Summary  This document reports on the paper submission and review process for the ICSE 2019 Technical Track, co-chaired by Tevfik Bultan and Jon Whittle. Highlights of the report are listed below.  \",\"source_role\":\"document\",\"source_message_indices\":[],\"time_metadata\":{}}}]},\"cards\":[]},\"original_question\":\"When and where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"task\":{\"question\":\"Where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"output_slot\":\"agreement_location\",\"answer_type\":\"text\",\"inputs\":[],\"execution\":\"retrieval\"},\"grounded_subquestion\":\"Where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"request_context\":{\"query_identity\":\"46754e5d1bfe94f3069272571aa2de1e4f4ae699beb1c08a82251f9d94756933\",\"algorithm_version\":\"dagbt_joint_memory_v1\",\"program_identity\":\"bc974c3f05127afd571e8b2de0613711721b8d735b15b68fe1b4c4bfaea288f2\",\"initial_dag_identity\":\"ed5e830eaf4e6a2a8abd283305d8543b705d810f6a5a03e189ae33c8f6ea234b\"},\"variables\":{\"location\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"demand\":\"agreement_location\",\"open\":true,\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}}},\"requested_slots\":[\"location\"],\"requested_scopes\":{\"location\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"output_contract\":{\"mode\":\"value\",\"result_type\":\"record\",\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\",\"explanation\":\"none\",\"fields\":[\"date\",\"location\"]},\"program_revision\":0,\"rule_binding\":null,\"supported_parents\":{},\"known_observations\":[],\"source_spans\":[[\"s35\",\"d1\",0,366,\"http://www.icse-conferences.org/sc/ICSE/2019/ICSE2019-PC-Chairs-Report.pdf\\nICSE 2019 PC Chairs’ Report Tevfik Bultan and Jon Whittle, ICSE 2019 PC Co-Chairs  Executive Summary  This document reports on the paper submission and review process for the ICSE 2019 Technical Track, co-chaired by Tevfik Bultan and Jon Whittle. Highlights of the report are listed below.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s36\",\"d1\",366,715,\"● The Technical Track received 529 submissions (one short of the highest of any ICSE), of which 21% of papers were accepted.  ● A very similar review process to 2018 was followed, using the two-tier Program Board (PB) and Program Committee (PC) model. The major change from previous years was that we did not run a face-to-face physical PB meeting. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s37\",\"d1\",715,1068,\"Instead, decisions on papers were made wholly online. Survey data from the PC suggests that this was well-received. There is no evidence of any degradation in the quality of accepted papers; indeed the statistics on various aspects of the submission and review process are very similar to 2018. ● “Heavy” double blind was used in a similar way to 2018. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s38\",\"d1\",1068,1467,\"We were less strict in re-assigning reviewers if author identities became known. This appears to be a welcome decision in achieving balance between anonymity and practicality. This is borne out by both survey data and informal comments from the PB/PC. ● Diversity in the PB/PC has always been a major goal of ICSE PC-Chairs. In 2018, in particular, a strong emphasis was placed on gender diversity. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s39\",\"d1\",1467,1796,\"This focus was continued in 2019: we achieved 50% female representation in the PB and 40% female representation in the PC. Generally speaking, geographic diversity was also achieved. However, given the growth in submissions from some countries, the ratio of submitting authors to PC members from a given country can vary widely. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s40\",\"d1\",1796,2021,\"Such data suggests that China is significantly under-represented in the PB/PC (not just in 2019).   1 Introduction and Background  ICSE is the largest and most prestigious publication venue for software engineering research. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s41\",\"d1\",2021,2315,\"Given its reputation and visibility, the primary consideration of PC Chairs is to maintain the high quality of the program while adapting to changes and growth in the field.   The ICSE 2019 Technical Track followed many of the same principles and processes that were successfully used in 2018. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s42\",\"d1\",2315,2539,\"We used the two-level review model: a Program Board of 34 experienced ICSE researchers moderated discussions and helped to maintain quality in the paper reviews, which were written by a team of 98 Program Committee members. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s43\",\"d1\",2539,2819,\"Reviewing followed the “heavy” double-blind review model where names of authors were not revealed until the final decisions (for accepted papers) or never (for rejected papers). The most significant change for 2019 was the introduction of a fully online review process: that is, \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s44\",\"d1\",2819,3128,\"instead of a two-day face-to-face meeting for PB members, we introduced an online two-day final decision-making period in which all PB and PC members were involved.  Below, we summarize the key similarities and differences in 2019 compared to the 2018 model: ● 2019 used the PB/PC two-level model as in 2018. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s45\",\"d1\",3128,3526,\"As with 2018, PB members were not expected to write paper reviews; their role was to moderate the reviews and the discussions, including suggesting improvements to reviews and bringing reviewers towards a consensus decision during discussions. Unlike in 2018, PB members were free to bring in their own views on papers - although, the recommendation was to aim to reach a reviewer consensus first. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s46\",\"d1\",3526,3876,\"● 2019 used a fully online review process; there was no face-to-face meeting of PB members. This culminated in a two-day online period for final decision making where all PC and PB members were expected to be available for 8 hours within the day (their choice, according to time zone). All decisions were finalized by the end of this two-day period. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s47\",\"d1\",3876,4211,\"All discussions, including those during the two-day online period, were conducted by posting comments via the conference management software (Easychair was used in 2019).  ● No submission of artifacts was allowed alongside paper submissions. Instead, a separate Artifact Evaluation Track was introduced, managed by independent Chairs. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s48\",\"d1\",4211,4553,\"Authors of accepted papers in the Technical Track were invited to submit artifacts to the Artifact Evaluation Track. ● As in 2018, the 2019 process made a commitment to authors that they would get the chance to provide a response to ALL reviews. Crucially, this meant that no further reviews were solicited after the Author Response period.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s49\",\"d1\",4553,4773,\"● As in 2018, all papers were given a meta-review, written by the moderating PB member, summarizing the pros/cons of the paper and justifying the final decision. ● The review form for 2019 was changed from that in 2018. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s50\",\"d1\",4773,5015,\"In particular, reviewers were no longer asked to score papers separately on Significance, Soundness, Novelty, Replicability and Presentation Quality since these scores were not considered to add significant value during the discussion phase. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s51\",\"d1\",5015,5377,\"On the other hand, an additional question was added where the reviewers were asked to rank their set of papers - for each paper, to say whether the paper was in the Top 15%, Top 16-30%, Top 31-50%, Bottom 50% of the ICSE submissions in order to help reviewers normalize their recommendations with respect to the overall selectivity of the ICSE Technical Track.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s52\",\"d1\",5377,5743,\"● The ICSE 2019 reviews sent to authors did not include the scores entered by the reviewers. Scores were included in the ICSE 2018 reviews sent to authors but not in prior years. Some authors raised strong objections to scores being excluded from the reviews.   ● The General Chair and PC Co-Chairs decided to introduce “blended” sessions for the ICSE 2019 program. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s53\",\"d1\",5743,6141,\"That is, papers were not always scheduled by track but by topic. Papers from the Technical Track, NIER, Demo Track, Journal First Track and SEIP were all blended in this way, meaning that a given session on a topic could have papers related to that topic from any of those tracks. It was felt that grouping papers by topic rather than track would lead to a better overall experience for attendees. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s54\",\"d1\",6141,6455,\"● The program also designated some papers as more industrially relevant. These papers were marked in the conference program as part of the “Industry Program”. \\nThe decision on which papers to include was informed by an additional question in the review form asking PC members to mark industrially-relevant papers. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s55\",\"d1\",6455,6822,\"Due to scheduling constraints, some Industry Program sessions also included papers not so marked - based on the General Chair/PC Chairs’ best judgment. One of the three ICSE keynotes was a SEIP keynote and also advertised as part of the Industry Program.   2 Process  2.a PB/PC Two-Tier Model The review process followed the two-tier PB/PC model from previous ICSEs. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s56\",\"d1\",6822,7104,\"Similarly to ICSE 2018, the role of the PB members was to moderate the reviews and the discussions, serve as discussants but not serve as reviewers. Hence, all reviews were written by PC members. The instructions sent to the PB/PC outlined the distinction between these two roles.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s57\",\"d1\",7104,7308,\"The responsibilities for PC members included:  ● Write detailed and high quality reviews of around 15 papers and submit them in a timely manner. PC members are required to do all the reviews themselves.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s58\",\"d1\",7308,7680,\"● Be responsive to requests from the PB and/or PC Chairs when the PB member asks for review quality to be improved, or when PC Chairs ask for additional reviews (which may be necessary in a small number of cases).  ● Actively discuss reviews online with other reviewers and the PB member. PC members are expected to be respectful, courteous and professional at all times. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s59\",\"d1\",7680,8012,\"The onus is on PC members to reach a consensus on accepting or rejecting a paper, so PC members are expected to actively work towards this consensus.  ● Be available for the online PB/PC meeting. Book 2 full days (December 3-4, 2018) for participating in online discussions (through Easychair) for finalizing decisions on papers.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s60\",\"d1\",8012,8167,\"The responsibilities for PB members included: ● Check scope/formatting/anonymity violations for around 15 papers. ● Oversee the reviewing of these papers. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s61\",\"d1\",8167,8563,\"This involves checking the reviews submitted by three program committee members to ensure that they meet the quality standards expected from ICSE reviews, requesting extra reviews when necessary, initiating and managing the on-line discussion and writing meta-reviews. ● Serve as a discussant for an additional 5 to 6 papers by participating in online discussion and helping to reach a decision. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s62\",\"d1\",8563,8933,\"● Be available for the online PB/PC meeting. Book 2 full days (December 3-4, 2018) for participating in online discussions (through Easychair) for finalizing decisions on papers.   In addition, PB members played two different roles, depending on the stage of the review process (see Section 2c): ● Discussion Leader: Read the papers and the reviews for assigned papers. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s63\",\"d1\",8933,9296,\"Lead discussions and act as arbiter if consensus cannot be reached. Write the meta-review. \\n● Discussant: Read the paper and the reviews. Participate in discussion and help reach a decision.    2.b Fully Online PB/PC Meeting The ICSE Steering Committee ratified a proposal to shift from a face-to-face PB meeting to a fully online PB/PC meeting (see Appendix A). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s64\",\"d1\",9296,9679,\"Briefly, the background to this decision began at the ICSE 2018 PB meeting. After many discussions on the pros and cons of having an online vs. physical PB meeting, we held a vote of ICSE 2018 PB members to get their opinion on this issue. We asked the ICSE 2018 PB members to answer ‘yes’ or ‘no’ to the question: “Would you prefer an online PB meeting over a physical PB meeting?” \",\"document\",[]],[\"s65\",\"d1\",9679,10033,\"The results were 22 in favour of an online meeting, and 11 in favour of a physical meeting, a two thirds majority. After further discussion with the ICSE 2019 General Chair and ICSE 2020 PC Chairs, we decided to propose a review process based on an online PB+PC meeting (Appendix A). We sent the proposal to the ICSE Steering Committee in February 2018. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s66\",\"d1\",10033,10354,\"After an online discussion about the proposal among the ICSE Steering Committee members, there was a vote and the proposal was approved in March 2018 (16 votes for and 6 votes against).  2.c Selection of the PB and the PC  One can argue that selection of the PB/PC is the most significant task performed by any PC Chair. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s67\",\"d1\",10354,10679,\"Our main goal was to select experienced researchers who have demonstrated excellent performance as reviewers and discussants in prior conferences as PB members, and to select researchers with a strong track record both as researchers and reviewers as PC members. We looked for significant prior PC experience for PB members. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s68\",\"d1\",10679,10912,\"For PC members we looked for recognized expertise as demonstrated by strong publication records in quality venues, while taking into account their seniority and research area (which can lead to variations in publication records).    \",\"document\",[]]],\"required_scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"},\"rules_completion_required\":false,\"supported_task_outputs\":{},\"source_documents\":{\"d1\":{\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"time_metadata\":{}}},\"source_span_columns\":[\"id\",\"doc_id\",\"start\",\"end\",\"text\",\"source_role\",\"source_message_indices\"]}"}], "logical_call_index": 11, "response_format": "plain", "input_tokens_local": 7152, "output_token_reserve": 4096, "input_margin": 256, "max_model_len": 120000, "tokenization_request_sha256": "5c5d1e1ec7c84392256d773f279c5434a69c0d349066d1f65384ed97cc1d0639", "tokenization_endpoint_sha256": "0ed12ee94b55bfdbc62fb247243af2d71aa1d7ea503f5f191605ae295c5ad94a", "token_count_is_estimate": false, "token_estimator_id": "deployment_vllm_chat_v1", "tokenizer_id": null, "add_generation_prompt": true, "tokenization_scope": "adapted_plain_text_chat_default_server_template", "token_accounting_note": "Deployment input count; model weights and tokenizer hash are not independently verified", "tokenization_wire_sha256": "fe22699776dc31b2b495b107de41d7dfa5f12d10c041814d49f726f278cd5392", "raw_output": "{\n  \"slots\": [\n    {\n      \"variable\": \"location\",\n      \"observations\": [],\n      \"unresolved\": [],\n      \"reading_status\": \"no_relevant_evidence\"\n    }\n  ]\n}", "response_metadata": {"finish_reason": "stop", "refusal": null, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 7